WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.058
- 15 years ago, if you
thought it was a good idea

00:00:04.058 --> 00:00:06.702
to pay strange men to drive around

00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:09.468
your fourteen-year-old daughter,

00:00:09.468 --> 00:00:11.091
you would be laughed out of a room.

00:00:11.091 --> 00:00:13.195
Now, parents are more
comfortable with their daughters

00:00:13.195 --> 00:00:16.207
going into uber than driving themselves.

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:19.403
We are going through major
major consumer shifts.

00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:21.652
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:21.652 --> 00:00:23.089
You got your perspective.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:24.075
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:26.702
(audience cheering)

00:00:26.702 --> 00:00:29.294
Don't you wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:29.294 --> 00:00:31.653
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:31.653 --> 00:00:34.500
Good morning, I'm really
excited to be here

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.697
for many different reasons.

00:00:36.697 --> 00:00:38.822
One, we have a good amount
of time for this keynote

00:00:38.822 --> 00:00:40.954
so I'm gonna be able
to create some context.

00:00:40.954 --> 00:00:43.828
Two, I've had the great pleasure,

00:00:43.828 --> 00:00:46.828
I run a 1000 person marketing agency

00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:51.817
and Chase Bank has been a
great client for a long time

00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:53.187
and I've been very close to that account.

00:00:53.187 --> 00:00:56.323
And even before that, had
worked with a bunch of people

00:00:56.323 --> 00:00:58.773
in the credit union industry
on their marketing strategies,

00:00:58.773 --> 00:01:02.050
so I always enjoy when
I have much more context

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.128
of the day to day.

00:01:04.128 --> 00:01:05.893
So that's a lot of fun.

00:01:05.893 --> 00:01:09.428
And three, I had an incredible breakfast

00:01:09.428 --> 00:01:11.945
with a small group of people
that are in the audience

00:01:11.945 --> 00:01:14.240
and just the quality of the questions

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.579
and the vibe in general excite me.

00:01:16.579 --> 00:01:18.420
So, thank you for having me this morning,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.921
thanks for being up.

00:01:19.921 --> 00:01:23.088
I got in late last night from New York

00:01:24.013 --> 00:01:26.647
and noticed a bunch of you
were still running around

00:01:26.647 --> 00:01:27.595
a little bit later.

00:01:27.595 --> 00:01:32.595
And so the fact that you're
here this morning is impressive.

00:01:32.866 --> 00:01:34.206
Many different places where I wanna go,

00:01:34.206 --> 00:01:36.295
one, I'm gonna give you a little
context of how it got here

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:37.937
because I'm gonna assume a
lot of you don't know who I am

00:01:37.937 --> 00:01:40.701
but number two, I think a
great way to start this talk

00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:42.626
is very simply this.

00:01:42.626 --> 00:01:44.509
You're about to find out that I grew up

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:46.520
in the liquor business and launched one

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.243
of the first e-commerce wine businesses.

00:01:48.243 --> 00:01:51.100
So I grew up in an
unbelievably regulated business

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.463
my entire career.

00:01:52.463 --> 00:01:54.330
I've had the great privilege of working

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.950
in the financial sector for
some of the biggest clients

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.454
in the world over the last half decade.

00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:03.992
And the thing that
keeps ringing in my mind

00:02:03.992 --> 00:02:06.556
as I was getting up this morning is,

00:02:06.556 --> 00:02:10.639
I think in 2019, as we
head into the next decade,

00:02:13.487 --> 00:02:15.581
I think this needs to be the conference,

00:02:15.581 --> 00:02:17.181
this needs to be the moment,

00:02:17.181 --> 00:02:19.785
this needs to be the time
that this collective room

00:02:19.785 --> 00:02:23.731
starts really putting
their mind in this place

00:02:23.731 --> 00:02:28.471
where they realize that
using regulation as an excuse

00:02:28.471 --> 00:02:32.633
to not innovate is not
a sustainable strategy.

00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:36.779
I think the reality is that
so many people in this sector

00:02:36.779 --> 00:02:41.036
default into blaming
regulation as an excuse

00:02:41.036 --> 00:02:44.673
to not learn the new communication portals

00:02:44.673 --> 00:02:46.910
that matter to our end consumer.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.650
I stand here today, and
the reason I started

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.480
a marketing company a decade
ago was because marketing

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.732
for me a decade ago after
investing in Facebook and Twitter

00:02:55.732 --> 00:02:59.352
and Tumblr, after being
an early investor in Uber

00:02:59.352 --> 00:03:03.852
and being a big better on
Netflix when it went public,

00:03:05.149 --> 00:03:08.700
I genuinely believed that
there are so many innovations

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.522
upon us whether it's blockchain,
whether it's peer-to-peer,

00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:14.404
whether it's online competitors.

00:03:14.404 --> 00:03:17.178
There's so much innovation
from a business standpoint.

00:03:17.178 --> 00:03:20.189
It's so difficult for so many
people here to go back home

00:03:20.189 --> 00:03:22.734
and really rejigger their app

00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:25.976
or their website to be
lacking the friction

00:03:25.976 --> 00:03:28.268
needed to compete in a 2020 world,

00:03:28.268 --> 00:03:30.377
this is like practical
in the trenches stuff.

00:03:30.377 --> 00:03:35.377
But the one place that is very
practical to begin the battle

00:03:35.836 --> 00:03:38.372
that is needed for the
companies and organizations here

00:03:38.372 --> 00:03:41.409
to compete in the next
year let alone next decade,

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:43.446
the one place you can adjust quickly

00:03:43.446 --> 00:03:46.276
is your marketing strategy
and marketing spend.

00:03:46.276 --> 00:03:48.107
It is hard to go back and rebuild

00:03:48.107 --> 00:03:50.917
a tech stack infrastructure,
it is much easier

00:03:50.917 --> 00:03:53.831
to go back and cut your
direct mail budget in half

00:03:53.831 --> 00:03:55.926
then allocate it somewhere else.

00:03:55.926 --> 00:03:59.109
This is a very important
time in this industry,

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:00.950
it's a very important time in our society.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:04.950
There's a real massive
naivete in the collective

00:04:05.872 --> 00:04:08.761
of understanding that the
Internet is the middleman

00:04:08.761 --> 00:04:10.484
and that everybody else is just waiting

00:04:10.484 --> 00:04:12.594
for their time to be exposed.

00:04:12.594 --> 00:04:17.076
Books got picked on first
but then along the way,

00:04:17.076 --> 00:04:20.246
I was saying this morning, just
only six or seven years ago

00:04:20.246 --> 00:04:23.315
I was in D.C. giving a
keynote to the black car

00:04:23.315 --> 00:04:26.088
and taxi industry desperately
trying to get them

00:04:26.088 --> 00:04:28.634
to understand that uber was a problem.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:31.386
And what everybody did in that conference

00:04:31.386 --> 00:04:34.298
is what everybody does in every industry

00:04:34.298 --> 00:04:37.340
which is they come up with
the excuses or concepts

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.762
that they hope the customer wants

00:04:39.762 --> 00:04:42.222
instead of the reality
of the customers needs.

00:04:42.222 --> 00:04:44.630
The reality is if you're in this industry

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.346
and you create any friction
towards a transaction

00:04:47.346 --> 00:04:49.765
for an end consumer,
they're not interested.

00:04:49.765 --> 00:04:51.221
We value time.

00:04:51.221 --> 00:04:53.723
So again back to my investing career,

00:04:53.723 --> 00:04:57.234
I invested nine years ago in Venmo, right.

00:04:57.234 --> 00:04:59.947
When I think about the
dynamics that that played out,

00:04:59.947 --> 00:05:01.350
and that one didn't work out as well

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:02.905
'cause it didn't operate to its success,

00:05:02.905 --> 00:05:05.416
it's sold early and in branch
tree really made the dollars

00:05:05.416 --> 00:05:07.242
but the thesis was right.

00:05:07.242 --> 00:05:08.938
And the reason so many of the things

00:05:08.938 --> 00:05:12.116
that have happened in my
career have worked out

00:05:12.116 --> 00:05:15.101
is because I'm blindly consumer centric.

00:05:15.101 --> 00:05:17.844
Anytime you think about
what's in it for you

00:05:17.844 --> 00:05:20.277
versus what's in it for the user of you,

00:05:20.277 --> 00:05:21.975
you're already vulnerable.

00:05:21.975 --> 00:05:25.443
And I do believe that we are
upon a very important decade

00:05:25.443 --> 00:05:28.258
of technology innovation
that only building

00:05:28.258 --> 00:05:31.484
your actual brand will be
able to compete against.

00:05:31.484 --> 00:05:34.511
We are only a decade away from at scale.

00:05:34.511 --> 00:05:38.136
So many of our customers
making financial decisions

00:05:38.136 --> 00:05:43.087
through the Alexa or Google
home or apple pod in their home,

00:05:43.087 --> 00:05:46.724
and when you understand what
that toll booth looks like,

00:05:46.724 --> 00:05:49.369
it's gonna make Google
search of the last decade

00:05:49.369 --> 00:05:51.437
seem like child's play.

00:05:51.437 --> 00:05:54.478
So let me take a step back
and give you a framework

00:05:54.478 --> 00:05:56.089
because I actually think
five minutes of context

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:57.983
of how I got here will help you understand

00:05:57.983 --> 00:06:01.378
what I'm about to push that
I think you have to consider.

00:06:01.378 --> 00:06:04.040
I was born in the former Soviet Union,

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.141
I came here when I was three years old.

00:06:07.141 --> 00:06:09.303
It was a super hardcore lifestyle,

00:06:09.303 --> 00:06:11.029
I mean I lived in a studio apartment,

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:14.017
half the size of the stage
that I'm standing on right now

00:06:14.017 --> 00:06:16.086
with seven family members.

00:06:16.086 --> 00:06:19.419
My dad got a job as a
stock boy in a liquor store

00:06:19.419 --> 00:06:21.602
and used to drive from
Queens to New Jersey

00:06:21.602 --> 00:06:23.847
for two bucks of an hour
which just tells you

00:06:23.847 --> 00:06:27.160
how cheap gas used to
be, and it was really,

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:28.417
it was a hardcore struggle.

00:06:28.417 --> 00:06:31.532
I went on one family vacation
in my whole childhood

00:06:31.532 --> 00:06:35.022
we finally moved to Ades in
New Jersey about five or six,

00:06:35.022 --> 00:06:37.938
four or five years in, six,
five years into America

00:06:37.938 --> 00:06:40.486
because my dad became the
manager of a liquor store

00:06:40.486 --> 00:06:42.528
in a town next door.

00:06:42.528 --> 00:06:45.290
That is where my
entrepreneurial career began.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.290
I started a lemonade stand and then,

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:55.067
I should be honest here,
I manipulated my friends

00:06:55.067 --> 00:06:59.124
to stand behind the other five
lemonade stands I created.

00:06:59.124 --> 00:07:01.123
So I had a six lemonade stand franchise

00:07:01.123 --> 00:07:02.862
(audience laughing)

00:07:02.862 --> 00:07:05.666
and for everybody here over the age of 38,

00:07:05.666 --> 00:07:08.183
you might remember big
wheels, those little things

00:07:08.183 --> 00:07:09.725
that we used to ride as kids.

00:07:09.725 --> 00:07:13.080
I used to ride my big wheels
to every one of my locations

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.242
at the end of the day to collect my cash

00:07:15.242 --> 00:07:17.959
like I was Tony Soprano or something.

00:07:17.959 --> 00:07:19.204
(audience laughing)

00:07:19.204 --> 00:07:21.871
So that's who you have on stage.

00:07:22.814 --> 00:07:24.619
Actually, now I'm gonna go...

00:07:24.619 --> 00:07:25.770
I appreciate the laughs,

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.271
so I'm actually gonna
really bring it to you.

00:07:27.271 --> 00:07:29.562
Actually, six months before that business,

00:07:29.562 --> 00:07:33.934
my first actual business was
going into people's yards,

00:07:33.934 --> 00:07:36.214
ripping their flowers out of their yard,

00:07:36.214 --> 00:07:39.142
ringing their doorbell and
selling it back to them.

00:07:39.142 --> 00:07:42.636
So not the proudest year of my life.

00:07:42.636 --> 00:07:46.304
But I was super entrepreneurial, I'm 43,

00:07:46.304 --> 00:07:49.767
so I had the benefit of being of the age

00:07:49.767 --> 00:07:52.288
when baseball cards and
sports cards got really big

00:07:52.288 --> 00:07:53.685
and was a huge phenomenon.

00:07:53.685 --> 00:07:56.899
And when I was 12 years
old, at that point,

00:07:56.899 --> 00:07:59.306
I'd already realized that
I was an entrepreneur

00:07:59.306 --> 00:08:01.597
even though every other immigrant
was getting straight A's,

00:08:01.597 --> 00:08:03.238
I was getting D's and F's.

00:08:03.238 --> 00:08:07.498
My mom is just amazing and
built an enormous self-esteem

00:08:07.498 --> 00:08:09.620
but she was super frustrated by that part,

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.784
so she really said that, if
you're not gonna be a student,

00:08:11.784 --> 00:08:13.229
you're gonna have to learn how to work.

00:08:13.229 --> 00:08:14.629
So I worked a lot.

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:17.418
But you know for me, this was not

00:08:17.418 --> 00:08:19.286
the golden era of entrepreneurship.

00:08:19.286 --> 00:08:20.983
Again, depending on what age you are,

00:08:20.983 --> 00:08:23.715
you know that the 80s and
90s was the college you go to

00:08:23.715 --> 00:08:26.870
is a direct attribution to how
successful or good you are.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:30.288
So I was making $2,000 to $3,000 a weekend

00:08:30.288 --> 00:08:35.015
at these malls in New
Jersey yet all my parents

00:08:35.015 --> 00:08:37.673
my parents' friends,
all my friends' parents

00:08:37.673 --> 00:08:39.631
and definitely all my teachers told me

00:08:39.631 --> 00:08:40.607
I was gonna be a loser.

00:08:40.607 --> 00:08:42.147
I don't know about you guys

00:08:42.147 --> 00:08:45.975
but when you have $37,000
in cash under your bed

00:08:45.975 --> 00:08:49.627
when you're 12 years old
and you're not selling weed,

00:08:49.627 --> 00:08:50.561
you're not gonna be a loser.

00:08:50.561 --> 00:08:54.233
(audience laughing)

00:08:54.233 --> 00:08:55.492
That one's for all the parents

00:08:55.492 --> 00:08:56.801
with D and F students out there.

00:08:56.801 --> 00:09:00.551
Don't worry guys, it
can work out, it's cool.

00:09:01.501 --> 00:09:04.549
Anyway, and you know it's
funny, the way I look at school

00:09:04.549 --> 00:09:06.541
in the modern education system and the way

00:09:06.541 --> 00:09:09.329
that college is structured
and the debt you have to incur

00:09:09.329 --> 00:09:11.495
to get a piece of paper
that no longer translates

00:09:11.495 --> 00:09:15.002
to an actual job that
makes it ROI positive

00:09:15.002 --> 00:09:16.842
is a lot of the way I think about

00:09:16.842 --> 00:09:20.217
being a traditional
financial service provider

00:09:20.217 --> 00:09:22.699
in today's environment
when you're not leaning in

00:09:22.699 --> 00:09:26.282
to the infrastructure
of the web let alone,

00:09:27.247 --> 00:09:30.303
and all of you know this
like, the amount of VC funding

00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:32.767
and private equity money
being put into FinTech

00:09:32.767 --> 00:09:36.138
and competitors and layers
above and like Cloud...

00:09:36.138 --> 00:09:38.452
We're not even talking,
forget about Bitcoin

00:09:38.452 --> 00:09:40.142
or cryptocurrency, we're not even talking

00:09:40.142 --> 00:09:42.830
about the manifestation
of a mature blockchain.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:47.543
There's so much pressure on
us collectively in the macro,

00:09:47.543 --> 00:09:51.051
what I'm affected by is
the next part of my story.

00:09:51.051 --> 00:09:54.286
When I turned 14, my parents said,

00:09:54.286 --> 00:09:56.073
okay, the baseball card thing is a fad,

00:09:56.073 --> 00:09:57.627
which they were right about,

00:09:57.627 --> 00:09:59.720
you need to start working
in the family business.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:01.589
So I went from making $3,000 a weekend

00:10:01.589 --> 00:10:04.756
to making $2 to $3 an hour bagging ice

00:10:05.823 --> 00:10:07.937
in the basement of my dad's liquor store.

00:10:07.937 --> 00:10:11.437
So I call those the Dark Ages, I hated it.

00:10:13.459 --> 00:10:17.054
My dad ran his liquor store
like the Soviet Union,

00:10:17.054 --> 00:10:20.065
so his employees didn't
necessarily love him.

00:10:20.065 --> 00:10:23.292
So the 14 year old son that look nine

00:10:23.292 --> 00:10:24.583
really took the brunt of that.

00:10:24.583 --> 00:10:27.964
So it was a tough era but
eventually when I turned 16,

00:10:27.964 --> 00:10:29.832
I was allowed upstairs
'cause I was relegated

00:10:29.832 --> 00:10:31.948
to the basement and bagging ice

00:10:31.948 --> 00:10:33.250
and something amazing happened.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.457
I realized that people collected wine.

00:10:35.457 --> 00:10:36.627
Even though my dad's store was called

00:10:36.627 --> 00:10:40.339
Shoppers Discount Liquors,
we were in an affluent part

00:10:40.339 --> 00:10:44.318
of New Jersey, Short Hills,
Millburn, Summit, Livingston.

00:10:44.318 --> 00:10:46.212
Some dollars in the neighborhood

00:10:46.212 --> 00:10:47.380
and they would come to the store

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.473
and they would ask for
these expensive wines.

00:10:49.473 --> 00:10:51.609
Me being bored, started reading about wine

00:10:51.609 --> 00:10:53.349
and that connection 'cause I wanted

00:10:53.349 --> 00:10:55.160
to help my family business made me realize

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:56.884
there was something there, a passion.

00:10:56.884 --> 00:11:00.706
And so by the time I
was 18, no 18 year old

00:11:00.706 --> 00:11:02.816
should know as much about wine as I did.

00:11:02.816 --> 00:11:05.970
I was off-the-charts wine book smart.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:10.709
And then I went to school,
freshman year, September 1994,

00:11:10.709 --> 00:11:13.626
I'm in my dorm room playing Madden 94,

00:11:13.626 --> 00:11:14.913
dominating by the way,

00:11:14.913 --> 00:11:16.268
(audience laughing)

00:11:16.268 --> 00:11:17.272
and my friend runs in and he says,

00:11:17.272 --> 00:11:19.074
you have to come and see this.

00:11:19.074 --> 00:11:22.003
At this point, I had already
been really helping my dad,

00:11:22.003 --> 00:11:24.719
I had a lot of knowledge, I was
a good salesman on the floor

00:11:24.719 --> 00:11:28.613
and I was gonna open up 800
Shoppers Discount Liquors.

00:11:28.613 --> 00:11:31.283
I was gonna build the Toys
R Us of liquor stores.

00:11:31.283 --> 00:11:35.427
It's kind of how I thought
about it as a 17, 18 year old.

00:11:35.427 --> 00:11:38.231
Now I'm in a dorm room in September of 94

00:11:38.231 --> 00:11:41.683
and I get pulled out of
my room and I go look

00:11:41.683 --> 00:11:44.850
and I see and hear for the first time.

00:11:46.382 --> 00:11:49.299
For the under 25 under 30 set here,

00:11:51.411 --> 00:11:55.391
you missed one of the great
rackets in business history.

00:11:55.391 --> 00:11:57.423
America Online used to
charge three bucks a minute

00:11:57.423 --> 00:12:00.651
to be on the internet,
though they gave you a CD

00:12:00.651 --> 00:12:04.628
that gave you eight trillion free hours.

00:12:04.628 --> 00:12:05.571
It was the first time I saw it,

00:12:05.571 --> 00:12:07.593
I said something ridiculously stupid like,

00:12:07.593 --> 00:12:09.103
is this the information superhighway

00:12:09.103 --> 00:12:10.828
or whatever we called it back then?

00:12:10.828 --> 00:12:13.512
I had heard of it, but at
this point I'm 18 years old

00:12:13.512 --> 00:12:15.918
just to put into context,
and I'm sure this resonates

00:12:15.918 --> 00:12:17.163
with a lot of people in this room,

00:12:17.163 --> 00:12:19.623
I'd been on a computer
for 20 hours in my life

00:12:19.623 --> 00:12:21.915
'cause of classes, it was just not a part

00:12:21.915 --> 00:12:23.204
of my lexicon or my life.

00:12:23.204 --> 00:12:24.902
But what happened next
was super interesting.

00:12:24.902 --> 00:12:26.760
Within an hour of being on the internet,

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:27.818
but the first time in my life,

00:12:27.818 --> 00:12:31.113
I found myself on a bulletin
board selling sports cards

00:12:31.113 --> 00:12:32.966
and I realized that this was going to be

00:12:32.966 --> 00:12:34.271
what I was going to do.

00:12:34.271 --> 00:12:38.429
And so on intuition and just tasting it,

00:12:38.429 --> 00:12:41.742
I launched one of the first
three e-commerce wine businesses

00:12:41.742 --> 00:12:45.097
in America, 1996, when I
launched winelibrary.com

00:12:45.097 --> 00:12:47.557
and rebranded my family's business.

00:12:47.557 --> 00:12:49.168
This is where it gets important here.

00:12:49.168 --> 00:12:52.017
My objective here today is to not worry

00:12:52.017 --> 00:12:56.074
about the macro issues potentially
over the next two decades

00:12:56.074 --> 00:12:58.156
because for a lot of
executives and operators here

00:12:58.156 --> 00:13:00.450
you won't be around to
see that play out anyway

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:01.767
and that's not practical,

00:13:01.767 --> 00:13:04.468
and I have no interest
in ideology or futurism,

00:13:04.468 --> 00:13:06.749
I care about general practicality.

00:13:06.749 --> 00:13:09.868
I believe the biggest ROI
that I can bring here today

00:13:09.868 --> 00:13:12.560
is help people under
and alternatives of what

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:14.243
they could be spending their money on

00:13:14.243 --> 00:13:17.567
to communicate to end consumers
to do business with them.

00:13:17.567 --> 00:13:19.733
That is what I'm passionate
about on everyday basis.

00:13:19.733 --> 00:13:21.229
I have no emotion, you'll hear me talk

00:13:21.229 --> 00:13:23.269
about LinkedIn and Facebook and Twitter

00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:25.488
and YouTube and a bunch
of things in a little bit,

00:13:25.488 --> 00:13:27.113
I have no emotion to them.

00:13:27.113 --> 00:13:29.319
I could care less if they spoke in Twitter

00:13:29.319 --> 00:13:31.670
and YouTube are around tomorrow or not,

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:33.430
the only thing I do, and it's not up here,

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:35.240
but when you saw my slides earlier,

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:37.324
it says I day trade attention.

00:13:37.324 --> 00:13:39.823
The only thing I care
about on an everyday basis

00:13:39.823 --> 00:13:41.935
is where is the consumers attention,

00:13:41.935 --> 00:13:44.003
how much does it cost to get to them

00:13:44.003 --> 00:13:47.327
and how and what creative do
I need to put in front of them

00:13:47.327 --> 00:13:49.164
so I can get their business?

00:13:49.164 --> 00:13:50.568
But I'm not emotional about it.

00:13:50.568 --> 00:13:53.282
I built my family business this way.

00:13:53.282 --> 00:13:57.884
I took a business that was
doing $3.8 million in revenue,

00:13:57.884 --> 00:14:00.895
10% gross profit, so you can imagine

00:14:00.895 --> 00:14:03.046
how little money was left for marketing.

00:14:03.046 --> 00:14:06.411
I took a business that, in
1998 when I left school,

00:14:06.411 --> 00:14:07.806
and started operating my dad's business

00:14:07.806 --> 00:14:09.530
on the back of winelibrary.com

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:10.987
on the back of email marketing

00:14:10.987 --> 00:14:13.647
and then on the back of Google AdWords

00:14:13.647 --> 00:14:16.530
which were the two big
innovations having a website,

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:17.560
doing email marketing.

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:18.496
How many people here were
doing email marketing

00:14:18.496 --> 00:14:20.291
in the late 90s, early 2000s?

00:14:20.291 --> 00:14:22.118
Raise your hands.

00:14:22.118 --> 00:14:24.256
So for the OGs that
just raised their hands,

00:14:24.256 --> 00:14:25.188
(audience laughing)

00:14:25.188 --> 00:14:27.338
do you remember those open rates?

00:14:27.338 --> 00:14:28.855
I mean I had an email newsletter...

00:14:28.855 --> 00:14:31.187
How many people have done email
marketing in their career?

00:14:31.187 --> 00:14:33.127
Raise your hands high.

00:14:33.127 --> 00:14:34.485
Wait to hear this.

00:14:34.485 --> 00:14:38.297
In 1998, I had a 200,000 person newsletter

00:14:38.297 --> 00:14:40.383
that had 91% open rates.

00:14:40.383 --> 00:14:42.409
(audience laughing)

00:14:42.409 --> 00:14:45.419
What I know more than the
sun will come up tomorrow

00:14:45.419 --> 00:14:48.252
is I know that marketers ruin everything.

00:14:48.252 --> 00:14:49.990
(audience laughing)

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:52.648
As a marketer, I ruined email.

00:14:52.648 --> 00:14:55.469
I remember my email newsletter
when I first innovated,

00:14:55.469 --> 00:14:57.076
I'm like, maybe I can email people.

00:14:57.076 --> 00:15:00.206
And so I emailed like 80 people
and we sold way more wine.

00:15:00.206 --> 00:15:01.380
And then I was like, wait a minute,

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:03.380
that didn't cost me anything.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:06.297
Like the catalog that I used
to send out the same to sell,

00:15:06.297 --> 00:15:07.994
the same stuff cost me a fortune.

00:15:07.994 --> 00:15:11.745
I remember from 1998 to
2000, sending so much email

00:15:11.745 --> 00:15:13.319
'cause I was convinced that eventually

00:15:13.319 --> 00:15:14.916
we would charge for it.

00:15:14.916 --> 00:15:16.888
Like it was so remarkable.

00:15:16.888 --> 00:15:19.094
And I went from emailing once a week

00:15:19.094 --> 00:15:22.023
to three times a week to five days a week.

00:15:22.023 --> 00:15:25.559
And then two years later, I
was like people read email

00:15:25.559 --> 00:15:29.654
on Saturday and Sunday,
so we went to seven.

00:15:29.654 --> 00:15:32.923
And then I was like, maybe you can send

00:15:32.923 --> 00:15:35.063
more than one email a day.

00:15:35.063 --> 00:15:36.563
(audience laughing)

00:15:36.563 --> 00:15:39.151
Systematically, from 1998 to 2007,

00:15:39.151 --> 00:15:40.734
I destroyed our email newsletter.

00:15:40.734 --> 00:15:42.009
(audience laughing)

00:15:42.009 --> 00:15:45.179
But on the back of that
and on the back of buying

00:15:45.179 --> 00:15:48.277
all the wine terms on Google
AdWords a day it came out,

00:15:48.277 --> 00:15:50.415
how many people here have
done Google ad marketing

00:15:50.415 --> 00:15:51.248
in their lives?

00:15:51.248 --> 00:15:53.921
So the day came out, it
was five cents a click

00:15:53.921 --> 00:15:56.935
for about 4/1/2 months before
10 cents became the base.

00:15:56.935 --> 00:15:58.561
I literally owned every word,

00:15:58.561 --> 00:16:01.051
from wine to Cabernet, to Merlot.

00:16:01.051 --> 00:16:04.376
This is where the story of
my career is devastating.

00:16:04.376 --> 00:16:08.209
I'm very proud that I built
my family liquor business

00:16:08.209 --> 00:16:11.788
from a 3.8 to a 60 million
dollar business in seven years

00:16:11.788 --> 00:16:14.296
with no capital, with no VC money.

00:16:14.296 --> 00:16:15.893
As a matter of fact, and you'll love this,

00:16:15.893 --> 00:16:18.369
this crowd will love this,
my dad's so old-school,

00:16:18.369 --> 00:16:21.936
I didn't even know that
credit lines existed.

00:16:21.936 --> 00:16:24.072
So I built a business
from three to 60 million

00:16:24.072 --> 00:16:26.533
on making every penny work its ass off.

00:16:26.533 --> 00:16:28.458
That was email and Google AdWords.

00:16:28.458 --> 00:16:30.173
That's what I'm gonna
talk to you about today.

00:16:30.173 --> 00:16:32.381
How do you make every
penny work it's ass off?

00:16:32.381 --> 00:16:34.702
How do you not, and I think everybody

00:16:34.702 --> 00:16:36.347
will resonate with this following line,

00:16:36.347 --> 00:16:38.524
the greatest thing that's
happening in marketing right now

00:16:38.524 --> 00:16:41.015
is that we put the past on a pedestal

00:16:41.015 --> 00:16:44.111
and we demonize the current.

00:16:44.111 --> 00:16:47.304
We put the past on a
pedestal, tried and true.

00:16:47.304 --> 00:16:51.387
We create bullshit reporting
to justify the spend

00:16:52.751 --> 00:16:55.539
and then we ask stuff that has real data

00:16:55.539 --> 00:16:58.198
to prove that it works.

00:16:58.198 --> 00:17:00.520
That is ludicrous and
that is why we're seeing

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:03.448
so many companies lose
market share and decline.

00:17:03.448 --> 00:17:06.195
It's because the executives
behind that feel comfortable

00:17:06.195 --> 00:17:08.345
with what got them there
instead of understanding

00:17:08.345 --> 00:17:10.409
that's exactly what's
gonna make them vulnerable

00:17:10.409 --> 00:17:13.425
and that you should be
required to continue

00:17:13.425 --> 00:17:15.575
to up your game and learn new skills

00:17:15.575 --> 00:17:16.904
and understand how new things...

00:17:16.904 --> 00:17:17.922
People are like, Gary
but I don't understand

00:17:17.922 --> 00:17:19.099
how Facebook works!

00:17:19.099 --> 00:17:20.908
I'm like, figure it out, dick.

00:17:20.908 --> 00:17:23.908
(audience laughing)

00:17:27.284 --> 00:17:29.558
Like, isn't that business,

00:17:29.558 --> 00:17:31.913
like isn't that the merit
of what we're doing here?

00:17:31.913 --> 00:17:34.096
Like the reality is,
that's what's happening.

00:17:34.096 --> 00:17:36.090
So I built up that business

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:40.123
on the back of those two
platforms, that was my life,

00:17:40.123 --> 00:17:41.425
that's kind of how I did it.

00:17:41.425 --> 00:17:44.681
And then a very transcending
moment happened to my career,

00:17:44.681 --> 00:17:46.662
and then we'll go into tactics.

00:17:46.662 --> 00:17:50.341
YouTube came out, I'd already
built this huge business

00:17:50.341 --> 00:17:53.156
and I saw it and I'm
like, this is interesting.

00:17:53.156 --> 00:17:54.824
It was about four or five months old

00:17:54.824 --> 00:17:57.782
and I started a wine show
called Wine Library TV.

00:17:57.782 --> 00:17:59.635
I sat in front of a camera,

00:17:59.635 --> 00:18:02.027
drank four bottles of wine for 20 minutes

00:18:02.027 --> 00:18:03.514
(audience laughing)

00:18:03.514 --> 00:18:04.914
gave my opinions about it

00:18:04.914 --> 00:18:06.529
and hundreds of thousands of people

00:18:06.529 --> 00:18:08.140
decided to start watching it.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:09.415
A couple funny things about it,

00:18:09.415 --> 00:18:10.955
and this is the number one thing,

00:18:10.955 --> 00:18:13.573
and we just had this at the
table, right before I came here,

00:18:13.573 --> 00:18:15.301
the reason most people struggle

00:18:15.301 --> 00:18:17.592
with contemporary digital marketing

00:18:17.592 --> 00:18:19.531
is because they're in sales mode

00:18:19.531 --> 00:18:22.418
not providing value and
building brand mode.

00:18:22.418 --> 00:18:23.874
You're transactional.

00:18:23.874 --> 00:18:26.987
It's like, what did this
post get me, its CAC and LTV,

00:18:26.987 --> 00:18:31.697
it's math, it has no brand
or LTV dynamics in it.

00:18:31.697 --> 00:18:33.112
I was that person.

00:18:33.112 --> 00:18:35.052
Remember the reason I told you is think

00:18:35.052 --> 00:18:37.074
about where my DNA
started, rip the flowers,

00:18:37.074 --> 00:18:39.665
sell it, right, not good.

00:18:39.665 --> 00:18:40.706
Well, great business models,

00:18:40.706 --> 00:18:42.733
sat on inventory for no time, high margin

00:18:42.733 --> 00:18:44.050
(audience laughing)

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:45.717
but not good morals.

00:18:49.833 --> 00:18:52.833
What I thought on February 21st 2006

00:18:54.532 --> 00:18:56.784
which is when I first taped
the episode of Wine Library TV,

00:18:56.784 --> 00:19:00.924
what I thought I was gonna
do was do QVC, right.

00:19:00.924 --> 00:19:04.418
How many people here grew up
in the New York tri-state area

00:19:04.418 --> 00:19:05.621
back in the day?

00:19:05.621 --> 00:19:08.636
How many of you remember Crazy Eddie?

00:19:08.636 --> 00:19:11.776
I thought I was gonna do
Crazy Eddie Meets QVC.

00:19:11.776 --> 00:19:12.880
I was gonna be ridiculous in myself

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:14.153
and I was gonna sell wine.

00:19:14.153 --> 00:19:17.988
The camera goes on, this
is literally five seconds,

00:19:17.988 --> 00:19:19.631
said if you literally go to YouTube

00:19:19.631 --> 00:19:21.923
and type in Wine Library TV episode one,

00:19:21.923 --> 00:19:24.229
I can see it, I know myself, I don't know

00:19:24.229 --> 00:19:25.235
if you'd pick up on it
but somewhere around

00:19:25.235 --> 00:19:28.076
one minute into the video,
I'm like, wait a minute,

00:19:28.076 --> 00:19:31.317
this is being recorded and
will be on the internet

00:19:31.317 --> 00:19:34.091
and will be around forever.

00:19:34.091 --> 00:19:38.109
I can't sell, I have to
actually give my actual opinion

00:19:38.109 --> 00:19:39.283
on these wines.

00:19:39.283 --> 00:19:41.415
And so the reason I became
an internet phenomenon

00:19:41.415 --> 00:19:45.052
and why it exploded was
I was literally going

00:19:45.052 --> 00:19:48.199
on the internet every
day, five days a week,

00:19:48.199 --> 00:19:49.953
to hundreds of thousands of customers

00:19:49.953 --> 00:19:53.829
and potential customers
and panning the wines

00:19:53.829 --> 00:19:57.218
that I was selling in
my own store at times.

00:19:57.218 --> 00:20:02.051
I was being an editorial voice,
not a transactional voice.

00:20:03.469 --> 00:20:05.789
What that seed created for me is something

00:20:05.789 --> 00:20:07.300
I wanna talk to you about,

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:09.254
but what's really
interesting to this story

00:20:09.254 --> 00:20:11.712
and how we got here today is YouTube

00:20:11.712 --> 00:20:14.545
sells to Google for $1.7 billion..

00:20:15.889 --> 00:20:19.085
I read an article, I'm super
blown away by that number.

00:20:19.085 --> 00:20:19.928
I don't know if you guys remember

00:20:19.928 --> 00:20:22.452
when that transaction
happened, that was 07,

00:20:22.452 --> 00:20:24.562
like it was a very
different world back then.

00:20:24.562 --> 00:20:28.469
1.7 billion feels like a trillion today.

00:20:28.469 --> 00:20:29.852
It just seemed ludicrous, 90% of the world

00:20:29.852 --> 00:20:32.923
had never even heard of
YouTube, it was this big thing.

00:20:32.923 --> 00:20:35.910
And in the article it said,
Angel investor Ron Conway

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:39.932
makes X amount of money
on his $25,000 investment,

00:20:39.932 --> 00:20:42.499
like 25 million dollars,
something ludicrous.

00:20:42.499 --> 00:20:44.974
And I remember thinking,
man, I've been so right

00:20:44.974 --> 00:20:47.265
about email and Google
AdWords and now YouTube,

00:20:47.265 --> 00:20:49.219
I need to become an Angel investor.

00:20:49.219 --> 00:20:51.989
And in 2007 and 2008,
I started doing that.

00:20:51.989 --> 00:20:53.860
And the first three
companies I invested in

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:55.940
were Facebook, Twitter and Tumblr.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:56.857
So I'm rich

00:20:59.278 --> 00:21:02.266
(audience laughing)

00:21:02.266 --> 00:21:07.221
but more importantly than
that is what happened next

00:21:07.221 --> 00:21:10.390
was instead of becoming
a VC, I mean you guys

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:12.767
know this dynamic on the
back of that and uber,

00:21:12.767 --> 00:21:14.609
like I wasn't rolling.

00:21:14.609 --> 00:21:17.437
And instead of raising two
or $300 million dollars

00:21:17.437 --> 00:21:20.407
on a fund, getting two
points, making all that money

00:21:20.407 --> 00:21:23.052
just to take meetings and
having 20% of the upside

00:21:23.052 --> 00:21:25.105
which is what most of my
friends did from that era,

00:21:25.105 --> 00:21:28.316
I decided to build a
20-20 version of Mad Men,

00:21:28.316 --> 00:21:31.624
client services, shitty
business, terrible yada,

00:21:31.624 --> 00:21:33.816
makes no sense to all my smart friends.

00:21:33.816 --> 00:21:36.986
But the reason I did it is
why I find myself here today.

00:21:36.986 --> 00:21:39.208
I believe there is a gross underestimation

00:21:39.208 --> 00:21:43.253
on how substantial the
consumer behavior shifts are

00:21:43.253 --> 00:21:44.653
and are about to be.

00:21:44.653 --> 00:21:46.339
If you think a lot of things have changed

00:21:46.339 --> 00:21:48.725
over the last five to
seven years, and they have,

00:21:48.725 --> 00:21:50.832
in the way we behave, in the way...

00:21:50.832 --> 00:21:54.749
I mean dating 15 years
ago, if you online dated

00:21:55.808 --> 00:21:59.552
you were the weirdest nerd
in your mom's basement.

00:21:59.552 --> 00:22:04.190
Now it is like standard to
swipe left and right 24/7, 365.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:08.572
Our behaviors in our
society, 15 years ago,

00:22:08.572 --> 00:22:12.041
if you thought it was a
good idea to pay strange men

00:22:12.041 --> 00:22:15.644
to drive around your
fourteen-year-old daughter,

00:22:15.644 --> 00:22:16.930
you would be laughed out of a room.

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:19.207
Now parents are more
comfortable with their daughters

00:22:19.207 --> 00:22:22.261
going into uber than driving themselves.

00:22:22.261 --> 00:22:25.540
We're going through major
major consumer shifts.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:27.370
And all the things that
were tried and true

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:30.420
are being pressured by technology.

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:32.830
This industry is on the precipice

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:35.010
of being disrupted dramatically.

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:37.440
Forget about macro technology trends,

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:40.490
just on competitors that
look like Amazon and others.

00:22:40.490 --> 00:22:43.020
It's all coming because
the services continue

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:46.170
to be commoditized as
the technology advances.

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:49.120
If you play out the chess moves of that,

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:51.360
it's the same thing that's
happened in politics,

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:53.890
it's the same thing that's
happening in transportation,

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:55.940
it's the same thing that's
happening to book stores,

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:57.440
the wine industry.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:00.380
What everybody in this room
eventually will realize

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:02.740
and I'd be very grateful
to get an email in 35 years

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:04.290
and be like huh, I
didn't really believe you

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.400
but now in hindsight, I'm
surprised how it played out,

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:07.430
you were on it.

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:09.260
I believe one man's point of view,

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:10.770
that if you play out the chess moves

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:13.310
of the advancement of
the inn and technology,

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:15.883
the only thing we are
all left with is brand.

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.810
The only thing we were
all left with is brand.

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:23.320
And why I decided to build
a client service business

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.110
and learn marketing was
I was an entrepreneur

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:27.840
in Silicon Valley winner
but I knew nothing

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.310
about fortune 5,000 land.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:32.420
I didn't understand why
the money was being spent

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:34.150
the way it was being spent.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.860
And I needed to go get my
hands dirty and taste it.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:38.860
And over the last decade, and ironically,

00:23:38.860 --> 00:23:41.310
today's the 10-year
