Headline readers versus practitioners.

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:01.750
People like to play hero on social media

00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:03.950
but then don't back up their actions.

00:48:03.950 --> 00:48:05.240
Their actions are very clear.

00:48:05.240 --> 00:48:08.300
Right this second, Facebook
is the single best place

00:48:08.300 --> 00:48:11.220
to market in the world because people

00:48:11.220 --> 00:48:13.830
are still not pouring
enough money on the platform

00:48:13.830 --> 00:48:15.700
because they're making emotional decisions

00:48:15.700 --> 00:48:17.650
versus practical business decisions.

00:48:17.650 --> 00:48:19.590
I could care less about Facebook,

00:48:19.590 --> 00:48:22.100
I have way more Snapchat
and Pinterest stack,

00:48:22.100 --> 00:48:24.900
things that I don't
believe are doing as well.

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:27.020
I wanna be historically correct
and I will stand up here

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:28.530
in 10 years and say,
you're still running ads

00:48:28.530 --> 00:48:30.080
on Facebook and Instagram?

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:31.740
They're overpriced.

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:35.810
What's great about Google
and Facebook and Instagram

00:48:35.810 --> 00:48:37.940
and Snapchat for that
matter and Pinterest,

00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:39.860
is it's a marketplace.

00:48:39.860 --> 00:48:43.760
Ads start at a penny, and
then supply and demand

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:45.620
for that attention goes up.

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:49.300
What's terrible about print
and radio and direct mail

00:48:49.300 --> 00:48:52.050
is there dying mediums, they're not dead,

00:48:52.050 --> 00:48:54.350
they're not doing as well as they used to

00:48:54.350 --> 00:48:56.930
and you're paying the premium.

00:48:56.930 --> 00:49:00.220
They keep raising prices,
that's a macro number

00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:03.710
you might be locally doing
but that's fine, I don't know,

00:49:03.710 --> 00:49:04.790
this is interesting to me

00:49:04.790 --> 00:49:06.913
as business operator spending money.

00:49:08.020 --> 00:49:10.200
We have such a passion for the past

00:49:11.170 --> 00:49:12.990
that we're willing to
overpay for something

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:15.950
we know doesn't work in lieu of something

00:49:15.950 --> 00:49:17.530
that's staring us in the face.

00:49:17.530 --> 00:49:20.960
and then we make fun
of Venmo or Robin Hood,

00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:22.830
Rock and More, AZebra.

00:49:22.830 --> 00:49:25.750
We make fun of these
things, we say these things

00:49:25.750 --> 00:49:27.870
and they're marketing a
completely different way

00:49:27.870 --> 00:49:30.180
and they're growing by the second.

00:49:30.180 --> 00:49:33.103
You're making fun of them
because you're not on their team.

00:49:34.030 --> 00:49:36.630
The second you quit this
job and go work for them,

00:49:36.630 --> 00:49:39.770
you're gonna sing its praises,
you have vested interest.

00:49:39.770 --> 00:49:42.520
It's like how I talked about
the Patriots, I hate them.

00:49:43.480 --> 00:49:44.740
They're cheaters, they're the worst,

00:49:44.740 --> 00:49:47.097
I hate everything, I hope
they all die them, fuck them.

00:49:47.097 --> 00:49:50.450
(audience laughing)

00:49:50.450 --> 00:49:53.173
I'm a Jets fan, that's how I roll.

00:49:54.460 --> 00:49:57.980
But that's sports,
that's me talking sports,

00:49:57.980 --> 00:49:59.340
that's not real life.

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:00.890
You're doing that in real life,

00:50:01.760 --> 00:50:03.280
you're making real life decisions

00:50:03.280 --> 00:50:05.300
based on not being on a certain team.

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:07.510
Guys, this has happened.

00:50:07.510 --> 00:50:09.480
You're not gonna get a
$100 million infusion

00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:12.010
into your tech stack, rebuild
your app and your website

00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:15.670
to create less friction
tomorrow but what you can do

00:50:15.670 --> 00:50:18.130
is take a real step back and really start

00:50:18.130 --> 00:50:20.810
auditing every penny that you spent.

00:50:20.810 --> 00:50:22.380
There's no reason to waste money

00:50:22.380 --> 00:50:25.290
because what I'm most
worried about is voice.

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:26.430
I will tell you it right now,

00:50:26.430 --> 00:50:27.590
I'm putting this on the record.

00:50:27.590 --> 00:50:28.640
Caleb, where are you?

00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:33.620
Caleb, I film all my content,

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:35.040
I like being historically correct.

00:50:35.040 --> 00:50:36.690
How many people here
follow me on Instagram?

00:50:36.690 --> 00:50:38.150
A couple, thank you.

00:50:38.150 --> 00:50:39.350
You can see in the last six months

00:50:39.350 --> 00:50:40.860
I've been doing the recall videos.

00:50:40.860 --> 00:50:42.870
Stuff I said in 11 that became true,

00:50:42.870 --> 00:50:44.610
this is my currency over everything,

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:48.100
so I love speaking for me,
could care less about me,

00:50:48.100 --> 00:50:50.200
the crowd, one thing, the truth.

00:50:50.200 --> 00:50:51.290
The current state of the truth.

00:50:51.290 --> 00:50:52.460
I don't sit up here and predict,

00:50:52.460 --> 00:50:54.100
everything you've heard in my mouth,

00:50:54.100 --> 00:50:56.180
it's things I've already seen happen.

00:50:56.180 --> 00:50:58.093
This is not guessing,
this is not futurist,

00:50:58.093 --> 00:50:59.360
this is not hoping.

00:50:59.360 --> 00:51:01.030
This is everyday being a practitioner

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:03.100
spending a billion dollars in ad money

00:51:03.100 --> 00:51:05.140
between my own agency and the
startups I'm involved with

00:51:05.140 --> 00:51:08.340
and my own companies and
watching how it all plays.

00:51:08.340 --> 00:51:09.630
By the way, one of the weird things

00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:11.390
to throw you for a loop,
I think the Superbowl

00:51:11.390 --> 00:51:12.650
is the number one thing everybody here

00:51:12.650 --> 00:51:14.190
should run in marketing.

00:51:14.190 --> 00:51:15.023
I think the Superbowl

00:51:15.023 --> 00:51:16.930
is the most underpriced move in marketing.

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:19.340
Everybody in America will
know what you're up to

00:51:19.340 --> 00:51:21.940
whether they watch it on
YouTube or during the game.

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:24.310
Another weird one, I'm starting
to really pay attention

00:51:24.310 --> 00:51:28.280
to the collapsing prices
of Drive Time Radio, right.

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:30.900
Everybody's listening to
podcasts and Spotify, I get that.

00:51:30.900 --> 00:51:34.613
To me it's not growing, I
just care if it's a good deal.

00:51:35.750 --> 00:51:37.820
By the way I'll buy a million
television commercials

00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:41.063
next year if they drop
their prices by 85%.

00:51:42.050 --> 00:51:45.740
So for me I just really I'm desperate

00:51:45.740 --> 00:51:47.890
for everybody to
understand how real this is

00:51:47.890 --> 00:51:51.310
because I've now lived more
than two decades of business,

00:51:51.310 --> 00:51:52.970
have yelled about this is gonna happen,

00:51:52.970 --> 00:51:54.220
this is gonna happen!

00:51:54.220 --> 00:51:58.020
People don't take it seriously
or not incentivized to

00:51:58.020 --> 00:52:00.030
or to care less or...

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:01.910
And it's tougher for employees.

00:52:01.910 --> 00:52:04.140
Back to when I used to
do it with entrepreneurs,

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:07.350
it was their family
business, that made sense.

00:52:07.350 --> 00:52:09.070
It's hard for you to fight a machine

00:52:09.070 --> 00:52:11.540
that you don't even own, I get it.

00:52:11.540 --> 00:52:13.730
But I will say this, and this is probably

00:52:13.730 --> 00:52:17.830
the single best thing I
can say in a room like this

00:52:17.830 --> 00:52:19.803
for the future of your career;

00:52:20.650 --> 00:52:22.890
The number one thing I am passionate about

00:52:22.890 --> 00:52:26.120
is for everybody in this
room to consider the value

00:52:26.120 --> 00:52:31.120
and the ROI of (mumbles),
for dying on your own sword.

00:52:32.810 --> 00:52:34.220
That was amazing that it went out, right,

00:52:34.220 --> 00:52:35.287
when I was about...

00:52:35.287 --> 00:52:36.120
(audience laughing)

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:38.340
That was so amazing.

00:52:38.340 --> 00:52:39.610
If you guys were doing that in the back,

00:52:39.610 --> 00:52:42.463
that was unbelievable,
big shout out to them.

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:46.060
The number one thing that
I wish for all of you

00:52:46.060 --> 00:52:48.300
is when you're sitting in a
meeting with senior executives

00:52:48.300 --> 00:52:50.200
or your marketing team or your agencies

00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:52.010
and you're talking about marketing,

00:52:52.010 --> 00:52:55.150
if you have an opinion
that you believe in,

00:52:55.150 --> 00:52:57.700
not because I said it, because
you actually went home,

00:52:57.700 --> 00:52:58.560
you know, if you're...

00:52:58.560 --> 00:53:01.130
How many people here are senior
marketers in their company?

00:53:01.130 --> 00:53:02.033
Raise your hands.

00:53:02.930 --> 00:53:04.290
For all of you that just raised your hand,

00:53:04.290 --> 00:53:07.990
if you don't go home and
spend 200 bucks on Facebook

00:53:07.990 --> 00:53:10.780
to try to sell something
that's sitting in your basement

00:53:10.780 --> 00:53:14.090
on Facebook marketplace,
it would be a huge mistake.

00:53:14.090 --> 00:53:17.580
You can't have opinions about
something you don't know.

00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:20.870
You must be a practitioner, please.

00:53:20.870 --> 00:53:21.910
So let me get to the point.

00:53:21.910 --> 00:53:25.720
If you're in a meeting tomorrow, next week

00:53:25.720 --> 00:53:27.730
and we're talking about
Facebook or Instagram

00:53:27.730 --> 00:53:30.980
or radio or television or
print and you have an opinion

00:53:30.980 --> 00:53:33.180
that's different than what
you as the professional

00:53:33.180 --> 00:53:38.040
is going along with, the ROI
of you voicing that opinion

00:53:38.040 --> 00:53:40.740
is that everybody else knows you said it.

00:53:40.740 --> 00:53:43.230
And it might not be valuable today

00:53:43.230 --> 00:53:46.320
but when everything hits
the fan, and it will,

00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:48.310
in three or four years, the people

00:53:48.310 --> 00:53:52.200
that were historically correct
will get the first jobs.

00:53:52.200 --> 00:53:53.670
That's real life.

00:53:53.670 --> 00:53:55.940
I've watched for too
long over the last decade

00:53:55.940 --> 00:53:59.490
friends of mine pander to the
companies that they worked in

00:53:59.490 --> 00:54:02.360
on things they didn't believe
only then to feel the wrath

00:54:02.360 --> 00:54:05.630
when the company got hurt and
they didn't have the resume

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:08.250
of articulating their
truth to protect them

00:54:08.250 --> 00:54:10.043
and so they lost twice.

00:54:11.300 --> 00:54:14.810
If nothing else, if
nothing else this morning,

00:54:14.810 --> 00:54:18.190
please in meetings talk about
what you actually believe in

00:54:18.190 --> 00:54:20.600
instead of what's convenient
for the short term

00:54:20.600 --> 00:54:22.789
because the haves and
have-nots of marketing

00:54:22.789 --> 00:54:25.210
are becoming very clear.

00:54:25.210 --> 00:54:26.513
The stakes are very high.

00:54:28.590 --> 00:54:30.340
Let me bounce here for
a couple more minutes

00:54:30.340 --> 00:54:31.240
while I've got it.

00:54:32.540 --> 00:54:33.930
One thing to really understand

00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:36.830
and why so many people are
struggling with marketing today

00:54:36.830 --> 00:54:39.430
is this is the greatest
era of math and art

00:54:39.430 --> 00:54:40.863
being equal partners.

00:54:41.810 --> 00:54:44.497
Way too many marketers today
either run too much quant

00:54:44.497 --> 00:54:46.650
or they run too much art.

00:54:46.650 --> 00:54:50.160
This world too much quant,
math math math, right,

00:54:50.160 --> 00:54:52.270
way too much, not building brand

00:54:52.270 --> 00:54:54.310
which is why voice is gonna be a problem.

00:54:54.310 --> 00:54:56.730
Voice is gonna be a problem
because everything we now do

00:54:56.730 --> 00:54:59.710
on our mobile phone over
the next decade or two,

00:54:59.710 --> 00:55:01.960
an enormous percentage of that activity

00:55:01.960 --> 00:55:06.110
is going to go to voice
devices because it's faster.

00:55:06.110 --> 00:55:08.210
The only thing we value is speed.

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:09.043
We give up...

00:55:09.043 --> 00:55:11.270
There is no privacy concerns in America,

00:55:11.270 --> 00:55:13.120
we prove it every day, we don't care.

00:55:14.100 --> 00:55:17.100
We care about convenience, we like time,

00:55:17.100 --> 00:55:18.610
we like lack of friction.

00:55:18.610 --> 00:55:20.410
It is faster for me to say, hey Alexa,

00:55:20.410 --> 00:55:22.250
I'll take two burgers and a fries

00:55:22.250 --> 00:55:25.423
than it is to find my phone
go to Seamless and order it.

00:55:26.980 --> 00:55:28.270
Many more transactions.

00:55:28.270 --> 00:55:30.170
I grew up as a kid that was told nobody

00:55:30.170 --> 00:55:32.200
would buy wine on the internet,

00:55:32.200 --> 00:55:34.410
that nobody would buy
tomatoes on the internet,

00:55:34.410 --> 00:55:36.970
that nobody would buy
a car on the internet.

00:55:36.970 --> 00:55:38.950
We buy all things on the internet.

00:55:38.950 --> 00:55:41.933
The Internet is real
life, this is fake life.

00:55:43.640 --> 00:55:47.430
And so please understand the
reason I'm pushing brand,

00:55:47.430 --> 00:55:49.570
the reason the Dwyane Wade thing mattered

00:55:49.570 --> 00:55:52.160
is 'cause building brand is
going to be the difference

00:55:52.160 --> 00:55:56.010
between somebody saying
Alexa, send me a case of beer

00:55:56.010 --> 00:55:58.163
and Alexa send me Budweiser.

00:55:59.420 --> 00:56:02.630
That is going to be everything
in the next frontier

00:56:02.630 --> 00:56:04.660
and if you're a young executive...

00:56:04.660 --> 00:56:07.310
How many people here are retiring
within the next 10 years?

00:56:07.310 --> 00:56:10.430
And I don't mean that
you're gonna crush it

00:56:10.430 --> 00:56:11.944
and go buy a plane,

00:56:11.944 --> 00:56:14.150
(audience laughing)

00:56:14.150 --> 00:56:17.673
I mean you're old and
you're kind of finished.

00:56:17.673 --> 00:56:21.070
(audience laughing)

00:56:21.070 --> 00:56:23.480
Great, great, okay.

00:56:23.480 --> 00:56:27.970
So for 9% of this
audience, I will say this;

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:31.000
Nothing I'm saying is
futurist, it all matters now,

00:56:31.000 --> 00:56:33.780
but obviously as you can imagine

00:56:33.780 --> 00:56:36.140
for the other 91%
percent of this audience,

00:56:36.140 --> 00:56:37.870
a decade from now, let me remind you

00:56:37.870 --> 00:56:40.600
that almost nothing we
talk about today existed

00:56:40.600 --> 00:56:44.200
a decade and a half ago, nothing, nothing.

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:46.020
This is gonna compoundly grow,

00:56:46.020 --> 00:56:48.350
it's gonna get way crazier and scarier

00:56:48.350 --> 00:56:49.750
if you don't like this stuff

00:56:51.100 --> 00:56:53.570
and I desperately need you
to understand that the,

00:56:53.570 --> 00:56:55.040
and this is actions over words,

00:56:55.040 --> 00:56:57.030
in a world where I could
have gone the VC route

00:56:57.030 --> 00:57:00.220
and had a lot of easy life and lived it,

00:57:00.220 --> 00:57:01.820
I wanted to build a marketing machine

00:57:01.820 --> 00:57:03.260
to take advantage of how big this is

00:57:03.260 --> 00:57:05.620
because I believe that
everything in our society

00:57:05.620 --> 00:57:07.263
is predicated on communicating,

00:57:08.570 --> 00:57:10.570
it's why dictators take over the press

00:57:10.570 --> 00:57:12.810
when they first have a coup.

00:57:12.810 --> 00:57:15.010
Communication matters, and we are living

00:57:15.010 --> 00:57:16.710
through the transition of communication.

00:57:16.710 --> 00:57:19.470
We have gone from a radio first world

00:57:19.470 --> 00:57:21.380
to a television first world,

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:24.650
we now actively live in a
mobile device world first.

00:57:24.650 --> 00:57:26.390
That's just where we are.

00:57:26.390 --> 00:57:28.373
And when you look at the mobile phone,

00:57:29.480 --> 00:57:32.380
the far majority of
activity besides gaming

00:57:32.380 --> 00:57:35.480
and entertainment is on social networks.

00:57:35.480 --> 00:57:37.490
If your business does not
know how to communicate

00:57:37.490 --> 00:57:39.550
on those seven to 10 platforms,

00:57:39.550 --> 00:57:41.993
every day your relevance
of brand is declining.

00:57:43.590 --> 00:57:44.660
This is very important.

00:57:44.660 --> 00:57:49.660
This is not ha ha ha fun,
this is massively disruptive.

00:57:50.430 --> 00:57:52.470
And the naivete that I've
seen from governments

00:57:52.470 --> 00:57:55.430
and politicians and other big
companies in different sectors

00:57:55.430 --> 00:57:58.100
is happening here right
now in this industry,

00:57:58.100 --> 00:57:59.300
I have a first view at it.

00:57:59.300 --> 00:58:01.010
It's why I love working
with Chase so much.

00:58:01.010 --> 00:58:02.560
Kristen Lemcke was one
of the most progressive

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:05.380
thoughtful CMOS period that I work with,

00:58:05.380 --> 00:58:06.900
let alone in this sector,

00:58:06.900 --> 00:58:09.100
pushing the envelope
around relevance of brand

00:58:09.100 --> 00:58:10.900
whether it's Sarena and Curry

00:58:10.900 --> 00:58:12.450
for that kind of cultural stuff,

00:58:12.450 --> 00:58:14.130
whether it's the tactics of eliminating,

00:58:14.130 --> 00:58:16.200
all those banner ads that we used to buy.

00:58:16.200 --> 00:58:18.320
She's on it, sharp as a tack.

00:58:18.320 --> 00:58:19.580
And she's in an ivory tower

00:58:19.580 --> 00:58:21.470
at the biggest companies in the world.

00:58:21.470 --> 00:58:22.900
Many people here are in the trenches

00:58:22.900 --> 00:58:25.270
should know can absolutely do this.

00:58:25.270 --> 00:58:26.840
I think you need to have a call in arms,

00:58:26.840 --> 00:58:28.170
this has to be the morning,

00:58:28.170 --> 00:58:30.360
we have to scrutinize
the money we're spending,

00:58:30.360 --> 00:58:32.400
we have to scrutinize the reports

00:58:32.400 --> 00:58:34.730
that we are held accountable
that everybody here knows

00:58:34.730 --> 00:58:37.240
don't actually match to
the reality of our business

00:58:37.240 --> 00:58:39.890
and we have to put pressure
on the most senior executives

00:58:39.890 --> 00:58:41.980
that sit on boards and C-suites

00:58:41.980 --> 00:58:45.506
that pontificate about
the past or asking for ROI

00:58:45.506 --> 00:58:48.030
within a minute of the spend

00:58:48.030 --> 00:58:50.160
because all those behaviors
that I just mentioned

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.550
are the singular reason
that great companies

00:58:52.550 --> 00:58:55.530
that make tons of profit
wake up seven years later

00:58:55.530 --> 00:58:57.270
and are no longer in business.

00:58:57.270 --> 00:58:59.830
Please, please heed this,

00:58:59.830 --> 00:59:03.802
it's coming 'cause it's
already here, thank you.

00:59:03.802 --> 00:59:04.946
(audience applauding)

00:59:04.946 --> 00:59:08.113
(bright upbeat music)

